THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL
--------------------

This work is a weight format and a set of kernels for llama.cpp / ggml, which
are distributed under the MIT License, Copyright (c) 2023-2024 The ggml authors.
MIT-licensed material may be incorporated into an Apache-2.0 work; the
attribution requirement below satisfies its terms.

Four files reproduce or adapt upstream code and say so in their own headers:

  benchmarks/i2s_tiled.h
      Reproduces the ARITHMETIC of tinyBLAS_I2S_AVX::gemm from
      ggml/src/ggml-cpu/llamafile/sgemm.cpp -- the same 2-bit unpack and the
      same vpmaddubsw sequence -- so that this project's format can be compared
      against the shape llama.cpp actually runs. Register allocation differs and
      the file explains why.

  src/ggml_t5b_glue.c, src/ggml_t5b_glue.h
      Implement the ggml-facing contract of the i2_s kernels: the same
      signatures, the same sum(code*a) return convention, and the same
      downstream post-processing (dot - act_sums[j]) * scale. The contract is
      upstream's; the implementation is this project's.

  tools/gguf_io.py, tools/gguf_inspect.py
      Parse the GGUF container format defined by ggml. The format is
      upstream's; the parsers are this project's.

No upstream source file is redistributed verbatim in this repository.

TWO CONSEQUENCES OF THAT, spelled out because both are visible to anyone who
clones this:

  src/ggml_i2s_ternary.c and src/ggml_i2s_ternary.h are NOT in this repository.
  They are GENERATED by tools/fetch_i2s_reference.sh, which extracts upstream's
  own AVX2 i2_s dot product -- verbatim, digest-checked -- from a named
  llama.cpp commit in a checkout the user supplies or the script fetches. The
  generated files carry the MIT attribution in their headers. Three of the four
  benchmarks and `gguf_to_t5b.py --verify` need them; the Makefile skips those
  targets loudly if they are absent.

  The integration is applied as a patch to an unmodified llama.cpp checkout --
  integration/t5b-integration.patch, with integration/apply_integration.py to
  apply it. The patch contains this project's added lines and the surrounding
  upstream context lines that a unified diff requires, which is the minimum a
  patch can contain; no upstream file is shipped whole.

MODEL WEIGHTS
